---
// Total public debt and debt-to-GDP ratio, Clinton through today (1993 – Aug 2026),
// with presidential terms shaded by party and major fiscal legislation called out.
---

<figure class="chart">
	<div class="chart-legend">
		<span><i class="swatch line-teal"></i>Total public debt</span>
		<span><i class="swatch line-warm"></i>Debt as % of GDP</span>
		<span><i class="swatch band-blue"></i>Democratic term</span>
		<span><i class="swatch band-red"></i>Republican term</span>
	</div>

	<div class="chart-canvas">
		<canvas
			id="debt-chart"
			role="img"
			aria-label="U.S. national debt from 1993 to August 2026, with presidential terms shaded by party. Total public debt rises from $4.2 trillion under Clinton to $40 trillion in August 2026. Debt as a share of GDP falls from 63% to 54% during the Clinton years, then climbs under every later president, spiking to an all-time high of 133% in mid-2020 and sitting near 123% today. Numbered markers flag major fiscal legislation from the 1993 deficit deal through the One Big Beautiful Bill Act."
		>
			Total public debt rose from $4.2 trillion in 1993 to $40 trillion in August 2026.
			Debt as a share of GDP fell from 63% to 54% under Clinton, then rose under every
			president since, peaking at 133% during COVID and standing near 123% today.
		</canvas>
	</div>

	<ol class="chart-key">
		<li><b>1</b> Deficit deal (OBRA-93) · Aug 1993</li>
		<li><b>2</b> Balanced Budget Act · Aug 1997</li>
		<li><b>3</b> Bush tax cuts · Jun 2001</li>
		<li><b>4</b> 2003 tax cuts · Medicare Part D</li>
		<li><b>5</b> TARP · Oct 2008</li>
		<li><b>6</b> Recovery Act · Feb 2009</li>
		<li><b>7</b> Budget Control Act · Aug 2011</li>
		<li><b>8</b> Tax Cuts and Jobs Act · Dec 2017</li>
		<li><b>9</b> CARES Act · Mar 2020</li>
		<li><b>10</b> American Rescue Plan · Mar 2021</li>
		<li><b>11</b> Fiscal Responsibility Act · Jun 2023</li>
		<li><b>12</b> One Big Beautiful Bill Act · Jul 2025</li>
	</ol>

	<figcaption>
		Total public debt outstanding: U.S. Treasury, Debt to the Penny — first reading of each
		month; the series begins April 1993, where Treasury's daily record starts, and ends
		August 18, 2026, the day the total crossed $40 trillion. Debt-to-GDP: Federal Reserve
		Bank of St. Louis (GFDEGDQ188S), total public debt as a percent of GDP, quarterly through
		Q1 2026; the final point is provisional — the August 18 debt level against Q2 2026 GDP.
		Bands mark presidential terms from each January 20 inauguration.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

